md/legion -- the no-PCI machine
===============================

The only machine that reaches the OpenBoot ok prompt on unmodified upstream
QEMU. Its disk is Sun's Legion simulator disk, driven by Sun's hsimd driver
(OpenSPARC T2, legion/src/support/hsimd, GPL-2). A standalone copy of those
files, byte-identical to Sun's, is at
https://github.com/artyom-tarasenko/hsimd

    make md  TARGET=legion        # default TARGET
    make all TARGET=legion        # obp niagara/t1_fpga, hv ontario/legion

QEMU command line
-----------------
    qemu-system-sparc64 -M niagara -L <outdir> -m 2048 -nographic \
        -drive if=pflash,file=<disk.img>,format=raw

    ok boot disk

The niagara machine maps the first IF_PFLASH drive as a RAM-backed ramdisk at
0x1f40000000 (NIAGARA_VDISK_BASE) -- writes never reach the file, so every
boot starts clean and readonly= is irrelevant. That 512 MB (or whatever the
image is) is on top of -m. A -drive without if=pflash is ignored: OBP then
finds nothing at disk@0 and `boot disk` faults (trap 0x32, MAXTL).

The guest
---------
The hsimd driver exists in practice only in Sun's experimental builds, such as
disk.s10hw2: a Solaris 10 guest boots from it. With more than 2048 MB of RAM
the guest uses 256M pages, which stock QEMU does not decode correctly; that
needs a QEMU patch, "target/sparc: implement TTE page size for 32M/256M
pages" (not in upstream yet; qemu-devel, 2026-09-18:
https://lore.kernel.org/r/20260918105705.433093-1-sun4qemu@gmail.com ; a copy
is in github.com/unix0cc/qemu-experimental-patches, upstream/). A CD image
attached as the disk boots too: OpenBoot reads it into a ramdisk, so the guest
needs no hsimd (boot /virtual-devices/disk@0:f).

How far a CD image gets depends on the Solaris release: older releases are less
restrictive about the machine description, newer ones more. The parameters of
these MDs are enough to initialise the kernel up to Solaris 11.2. Solaris 11.x
is out of scope now.

What is in it
-------------
The virtual-devices tree only: the Legion disk, the console (the qcn OS console
driver), nvram and the RTC. There are no LDC channels: the guest MD has no
channel-devices, channel-endpoints, vldc, vcc or vds nodes, and the hv MD has no
ldc_endpoint nodes. The hv MD does keep a bare channel_devices node under
guest0, because the hypervisor asserts on its cfghandle for every guest
(res_guest.c) and dies silently without it; the guest MD has no counterpart. The
disk node carries compatible = "SUNW,legion-disk", the OBP dropin that drives
it; that dropin is in niagara/t1_fpga and NOT in the pci OBP, which is why the
two configs have separate OBP targets (config.mk).

    disk  = /virtual-devices/disk@0
    vdisk = /virtual-devices/disk@0      second name for the same device

boot-device is "disk". platform.mac-address (0x8003dead03) is cosmetic --
there is no NIC on this machine.
